  "steps": [
    {
      "id": "s1",
      "short": "Two numbers",
      "title": "Two different questions",
      "chapter": "setup",
      "body": "[[Latency]]: how long does *one* request take, from leaving the user to the answer coming back? Watch one dot go out and return.\n\n[[Throughput]]: how many requests does the system finish *per second*? Count the dots.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "insight",
        "text": "Think of a highway: latency is how long your drive takes; throughput is how many cars pass per hour. Extra lanes let more cars through, but on an empty road they don’t get you there any sooner."
      },
      "changes": [],
      "metrics": {
        "p50": "120 ms",
        "p99": "180 ms",
        "throughput": "50 req/s",
        "util": "10%"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s2",
      "short": "Busier",
      "title": "More users, same server",
      "chapter": "problem",
      "body": "Traffic grows 7×. Throughput climbs with it — the server is still keeping up — and it’s now 70% busy: 350 of the roughly 500 req/s it can handle.\n\nBut look at the line in front of it: requests occasionally have to **wait their turn**. Waiting is latency too.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "Utilization = the fraction of time the server is busy. At 70%, a new request often finds it already working on something."
      },
      "changes": [
        "~ Users 1,000 → 7,000",
        "~ Throughput 50 → 350 req/s",
        "+ A short queue forms"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "p50": "160 ms",
        "p99": "600 ms",
        "throughput": "350 req/s",
        "util": "70%"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s3",
      "short": "Hockey stick",
      "title": "The hockey stick",
      "chapter": "problem",
      "body": "Traffic grows by another third and the server hits 96% busy. Throughput only rises to 480 req/s — a whisker under its ceiling of 500 — but the queue explodes and latency goes up **sevenfold**.\n\nRequests don’t arrive evenly spaced. With 30% spare, a burst is absorbed quickly; with 4% spare, each burst piles up behind requests that are already waiting, and the line takes ages to clear.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "warning",
        "text": "Latency grows slowly, then suddenly. Plan to run around 60–70% busy at peak and keep the rest as headroom."
      },
      "changes": [
        "~ Users 7,000 → 9,600",
        "~ Utilization 70% → 96%",
        "~ Queue 2 → 6 waiting",
        "~ p99 latency 600 ms → 4.2 s",
        "~ Throughput 350 → 480 req/s"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "p50": "900 ms",
        "p99": "4.2 s",
        "throughput": "480 req/s",
        "util": "96%"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "users-2": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "srv-2": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "q": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "u-2": {
          "visible": false
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s4",
      "short": "Averages lie",
      "title": "Averages hide the pain",
      "chapter": "insight",
      "body": "Back at 70% busy, most requests are quick — but a few get stuck behind slow work. The *average* here is about 180 ms, which looks healthy: it blends the slow few away.\n\nSo we measure [[percentiles]]: **p50** is the time half of requests beat; **p99** is the time only 1 in 100 exceeds.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "warning",
        "text": "A page that makes 30 requests hits the p99 on about 1 load in 4 (1 − 0.99³⁰ ≈ 26%). Tail latency is what users actually feel."
      },
      "changes": [
        "~ Back to 7,000 users",
        "+ Occasional slow requests (amber)",
        "~ p99 latency 4.2 s → 600 ms"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "p50": "160 ms",
        "p99": "600 ms",
        "throughput": "350 req/s",
        "util": "70%"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "u-2": {
          "effect": "ants",
          "emphasis": true
        },
        "users-2": {
          "emphasis": true
        },
        "srv-2": {
          "emphasis": true
        },
        "q": {
          "emphasis": true
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s5",
      "short": "Add capacity",
      "title": "Add capacity, buy back latency",
      "chapter": "solution",
      "body": "Double the server’s cores and the same 350 req/s is now only 35% of its capacity. The queue drains and both p50 and p99 fall.\n\nThroughput didn’t change — demand didn’t change. What changed is **how much headroom** there is to absorb bursts.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "To lower latency under load, lower utilization: more capacity, or less work per request."
      },
      "changes": [
        "~ Server 4 → 8 cores",
        "~ Utilization 70% → 35%",
        "- Queue gone",
        "~ p99 latency 600 → 190 ms"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "p50": "120 ms",
        "p99": "190 ms",
        "throughput": "350 req/s",
        "util": "35%"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s6",
      "short": "Distance",
      "title": "Distance is latency too",
      "chapter": "insight",
      "body": "Now move the server across an ocean. Nothing about it got slower, yet every round trip takes longer — watch the dots travel.\n\nLight in fiber covers about 200 km per millisecond. New York ↔ London is ~5,600 km: at least 56 ms per round trip, closer to 75 ms in practice.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "insight",
        "text": "No server upgrade beats physics. To cut this latency, move the data closer to users (caches, CDNs, regions)."
      },
      "changes": [
        "~ Server moved: New York → London",
        "+ ~75 ms per round trip",
        "~ p50 latency 120 → 195 ms"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "p50": "195 ms",
        "p99": "265 ms",
        "throughput": "350 req/s",
        "util": "35%"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "users-2": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "srv-4": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "chart-4": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "chart-4-text": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "u-4": {
          "visible": false
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s7",
      "short": "In flight",
      "title": "Little’s Law: how much is in flight",
      "chapter": "insight",
      "body": "A simple rule ties the numbers together: [[requests in flight = throughput × latency]]. At 350 req/s and 0.12 s each, about 42 requests are being worked on at any moment.\n\nEach in-flight request holds a thread, a connection or some memory. If latency rises to 1 s at the same throughput, 350 are in flight — and pools sized for 50 run dry.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "Use it to size thread pools, connection pools and worker counts — and to see why slow dependencies exhaust them."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ In flight ≈ 42 requests"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "p50": "120 ms",
        "p99": "190 ms",
        "throughput": "350 req/s",
        "util": "35%"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "users-2": {
          "emphasis": true
        },
        "srv-4": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "chart-4": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "chart-4-text": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "u-4": {
          "visible": false
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s8",
      "short": "Fan-out",
      "title": "One request, many servers",
      "chapter": "problem",
      "body": "A search page asks **100** index servers in parallel and must wait for the slowest. Each server is quick 99% of the time — but with 100 of them, a page usually hits at least one slow one.\n\n1 − 0.99¹⁰⁰ ≈ **63%** of searches wait on someone’s p99. A rare tail on one server becomes the common case for the whole page.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "Fix the tail with hedged requests: if a server hasn’t answered by its p95, send the same request to a replica and take whichever answers first."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ 100 index servers (6 shown)",
        "~ Page waits for the slowest",
        "~ p99 of one server → typical page"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "p50": "380 ms",
        "p99": "900 ms",
        "throughput": "350 req/s",
        "util": "40%"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "users-2": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "srv-4": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "chart-4": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "chart-4-text": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "u-4": {
          "visible": false
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s9",
      "short": "Batching",
      "title": "Batching: more throughput, a bit more latency",
      "chapter": "tradeoff",
      "body": "Writing analytics events one row at a time, the database manages about 2,000 inserts per second — most of the cost is per-statement overhead.\n\nGroup them: collect events for up to 50 ms and write 100 rows in one statement (the big squares). Throughput jumps 25×, while each event waits up to 50 ms longer.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "insight",
        "text": "Batching trades latency for throughput. Great for logs, analytics and bulk imports; wrong for a user waiting on a click."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ Events batched: 100 rows / 50 ms",
        "~ 2,000 → 50,000 rows/s",
        "~ Each event waits ≤ 50 ms"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "p50": "140 ms",
        "p99": "210 ms",
        "throughput": "50,000 req/s",
        "util": "35%"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "users-2": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "srv-4": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "chart-4": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "chart-4-text": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "u-4": {
          "visible": false
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s10",
      "short": "Recap",
      "title": "Recap",
      "chapter": "recap",
      "body": "• **Latency** is time per request; **throughput** is requests per second.\n• Latency explodes as utilization nears 100% — keep **headroom**.\n• Watch **p99**, not the average: the tail is what users feel.\n• **Distance** adds latency no server can remove.\n• **In flight = throughput × latency**; fan-out turns rare tails into common ones.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "Next up: **Napkin Math** — estimating how much capacity you need before you build anything."
      },
      "changes": [],
      "metrics": {
        "p50": "120 ms",
        "p99": "190 ms",
        "throughput": "350 req/s",
        "util": "35%"
      },
